dict(sec=2, img='splitFixed', cap='Timing of the heart sounds in expiration and inspiration in this patient',
stem="A 32-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 6-month history of fatigue and occasional palpitations. Her pulse is 78/min and regular, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. A soft systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left second intercostal space. The timing of her heart sounds during breathing, recorded by phonocardiography, is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely underlying condition?",
choices=["Atrial septal defect","Left bundle branch block","Mitral valve prolapse","Pulmonic valve stenosis","Right bundle branch block","Ventricular septal defect"], key=0,
exp="The aortic (A2) and pulmonic (P2) components are the <b>same distance apart in expiration and inspiration</b>: a <b>fixed split S2</b>. In an <b>atrial septal defect</b>, the left-to-right shunt keeps the right ventricle volume-loaded in both phases of breathing. Inspiration adds venous return from the body but cuts the shunt flow by a similar amount, so right ventricular stroke volume and P2 timing stay the same. The soft systolic murmur at the left second intercostal space is increased flow across the normal pulmonic valve, not the defect itself.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A fixed split S2 (unchanged with breathing) indicates an atrial septal defect.",
wrong={1:"Left bundle branch block delays aortic closure, giving a paradoxical split: P2 before A2, with the split narrowing on inspiration.",
2:"Mitral valve prolapse gives a mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur; it does not alter S2 splitting.",
3:"Pulmonic stenosis delays P2 and gives a wide split that still widens further on inspiration; it is not fixed.",
4:"Right bundle branch block delays P2, giving a wide split that still varies with breathing.",
5:"A ventricular septal defect produces a harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; splitting is normal or wide but not fixed."},
eli5="In an atrial septal defect, extra blood keeps sloshing into the right heart whether she breathes in or out. So the pulmonic valve always closes late by the same amount, and the split never changes."),

dict(sec=2, img='splitPara', cap='Timing of the heart sounds in expiration and inspiration in this patient',
stem="An 81-year-old man comes to the office because he fainted twice in the past month, each time while walking uphill. His pulse is 70/min and regular, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Carotid upstrokes are weak and delayed. A harsh, late-peaking crescendo–decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right second intercostal space and radiates to both carotid arteries. The timing of his second heart sound during breathing, recorded by phonocardiography, is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the pattern of the second heart sound?",
choices=["Delayed closure of the aortic valve","Delayed closure of the pulmonic valve","Early closure of the aortic valve","Early closure of the pulmonic valve","Equalized ventricular volumes from a shunt"], key=0,
exp="Exertional syncope, weak and delayed carotid pulses (parvus et tardus) and a late-peaking crescendo–decrescendo murmur radiating to the carotids are <b>severe aortic stenosis</b>. The left ventricle takes so long to push its stroke volume through the narrowed valve that <b>aortic closure (A2) is delayed past pulmonic closure (P2)</b>. The order flips (P2 then A2), and inspiration, which delays P2 as usual, moves P2 toward A2 and narrows the split. That is <b>paradoxical (reversed) splitting</b>. Left bundle branch block does the same by delaying left ventricular activation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Paradoxical splitting of S2 (split on expiration, narrowing on inspiration) comes from delayed aortic closure, as in severe aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block.",
wrong={1:"Delayed pulmonic closure (right bundle branch block, pulmonic stenosis) widens a normally ordered split, which still widens on inspiration.",
2:"Early aortic closure (as in mitral regurgitation, where the left ventricle empties quickly in two directions) widens a normally ordered split; it does not reverse the order.",
3:"Early pulmonic closure would narrow the split without reversing it, and nothing in his findings shortens right ventricular ejection.",
4:"A left-to-right shunt (atrial septal defect) produces a fixed split, unchanged with breathing; his split changes with breathing and is reversed."},
eli5="His aortic valve is so narrow that the left ventricle takes extra long to empty, so the aortic valve shuts late, even after the pulmonic valve. The usual order gets flipped."),

